<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sremski Karlovci</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Karlowitz, Karlovci</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sremski Karlovci is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> was signed there in 1699, and it became the seat of the Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> metropolitans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg Monarchy</a>. In May 1848 the May Assembly held there proclaimed the Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci.jpg" alt="Sremski Karlovci" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ванилица · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vidikovac_Sremski_Karlovci_8295_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sremski Karlovci stands on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a>, in the South Bačka District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, about 75 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. Its name in Serbian is plural. It is known in German as Karlowitz or Carlowitz, in Hungarian as Karlóca and in Turkish as Karlofça. The older Serbian form Karlovci is still used unofficially.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was first mentioned in 1308 under the name Karom. The medieval fortress of Karom was built on the ruins of a Roman one, and Hungarian noble families, among them the Báthory and Morović, held it until 1521. In that year the Ottoman commander Bali-beg conquered it, and it remained Ottoman for about 170 years. The Slavic name Karlovci was first recorded in 1532/33. An Ottoman survey of 1545 counted 547 Christian Serb houses, three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches and a monastery.</p><p>From 16 November 1698 to 26 January 1699 a congress at Karlovci ended hostilities between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a>, a coalition that included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a>. It was the first time a round table was used in international politics. Karlovci then passed to the Habsburg monarchy and its Military Frontier, and became the spiritual, political and cultural centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> there, with the Serbian Orthodox metropolitan in residence. The first Serbian gymnasium was founded in 1791, and an Orthodox seminary three years later.</p><p>At the Serb National Assembly in May 1848 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> declared the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranya">Baranya</a> into Serbian Vojvodina, with Karlovci as its first capital, and the metropolitan's title was raised to that of Patriarch. The Patriarchate of Karlovci operated until 1920, when the Serbian ecclesiastical provinces were united. From 1921 the patriarch's palace housed the synod of the Russian Orthodox Church Outside Russia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The municipality consists of the town alone, with 7,872 inhabitants at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2022. The Karlovci Gymnasium is the oldest secondary school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and the Saint Arsenije Seminary still operates. The Serbian Patriarch retains the title of Metropolitan of Karlovci.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sremski Karlovci” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Osijek's fortress, Tvrđa, was rebuilt in the 1710s and 1720s, and the bishopric of Đakovo revived Catholic life, while Orthodox Serbs were organised around <b>Sremski Karlovci</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-slavonia">Kingdom of Slavonia</a></li><li><blockquote>A national assembly at <b>Sremski Karlovci</b> in May 1848 proclaimed the Serbian Vojvodina in Srem, Baranya, Bačka and Banat, elected Josif Rajačić patriarch and Colonel Stevan Šupljikac voivode, and set up a Main Board led by Đorđe Stratimirović.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-vojvodina">Serbian Vojvodina</a></li></ul></section>
